WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.610
AI analysis for ejection fraction estimation from twelve-lead
ECG.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.812
From West Virginia University, published in Scientific
Reports.

00:00:11.262 --> 00:00:18.222
The distinguishing feature is the population: this is the
first study of its kind on rural Appalachian patients.

00:00:19.572 --> 00:00:31.764
Heart failure affects roughly 6.7 million American adults, and
the Heart Failure Society projects 8.5 million by 2030.

00:00:32.214 --> 00:00:42.990
The standard way to assess it is ejection fraction — the
fraction of blood the heart pushes out with each beat. A

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:49.824
Measuring EF means an echocardiogram: ultrasound imaging
interpreted by a trained sonographer.

00:00:50.274 --> 00:00:57.594
That is expensive, time-consuming, and in remote areas simply
hard to get to. Wait times can be long.

00:00:58.944 --> 00:01:02.664
A twelve-lead ECG is the opposite kind of test.

00:01:03.114 --> 00:01:11.514
It takes ten seconds, the equipment is cheap and ubiquitous,
and a wide range of healthcare staff can perform one — no

00:01:11.964 --> 00:01:16.092
For most patients the ECG already exists in the chart.

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:23.142
What it does not do is measure ejection fraction. There is no
reading on the trace that says EF.

00:01:23.592 --> 00:01:31.704
So the question this paper asks is whether the electrical
signal carries enough information about pumping function for a

00:01:33.054 --> 00:01:38.022
There is a second motivation, and it is the one that makes
this study distinct.

00:01:38.472 --> 00:01:49.896
Almost all prior work on estimating EF from ECG has been
developed and validated on urban populations, while heart

00:01:51.246 --> 00:02:02.838
The raw material is 12-lead ECGs at 500 hertz from 55,500
patients across 28 West Virginia hospitals, paired with echo-

00:02:03.288 --> 00:02:11.328
That yields 541,267 candidate ECG-EF pairs — but most are
unusable.

00:02:11.778 --> 00:02:23.922
Requiring the ECG to fall within sixty days of the echo cuts
it to 39,239. Beyond that window the ECG may describe a heart

00:02:24.372 --> 00:02:35.364
Label cleaning removes a little more: ranges like 40 to 45
averaged to 42.5, text entries standardised, impossible values

00:02:35.814 --> 00:02:49.638
What survives is 38,960 pairs from 12,197 patients, split into
training, validation and test — with a patient confined to a

00:02:50.988 --> 00:02:57.132
The preprocessing is worth following, because the signal and
the label go through quite different treatments.

00:02:57.582 --> 00:03:03.294
On the signal side: a ten-second twelve-lead recording sampled
at 500 hertz.

00:03:03.744 --> 00:03:11.328
During training a random 2048-sample window is cropped out of
it, so the model never sees the same segment twice.

00:03:11.778 --> 00:03:18.402
Gaussian noise of the same length is added, which the paper
reports improves robustness to real-world noise.

00:03:18.852 --> 00:03:21.276
Then the signal is normalised.

00:03:21.726 --> 00:03:33.630
On the label side, an echocardiogram within sixty days gives
an EF, often as a range like 40 to 45, which is averaged to

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.984
Only then do the two meet, at the model.

00:03:38.334 --> 00:03:45.678
EF is a continuous percentage, but the models are trained as
binary classifiers, so someone has to pick a cut-off.

00:03:46.128 --> 00:03:57.480
That choice is clinical, not arbitrary. An EF at or below 35
percent identifies candidates for implantable defibrillators

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:04.842
Forty, forty-five and fifty percent mark progressively less
severe boundaries for reduced ejection fraction.

00:04:05.292 --> 00:04:13.332
Most prior work fixes the threshold at 35 and stops. This
paper runs all four as separate experiments.

00:04:13.782 --> 00:04:21.846
That matters because moving the threshold also changes the
class balance — and at 35 percent the low-EF group is a small

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:34.332
On the left, the distribution of echo-derived EF values across
the cohort. Most patients sit in the normal range, with a tail

00:04:34.782 --> 00:04:44.238
On the right, what that means once you threshold it. At every
cut-off the classes are imbalanced, and the imbalance gets

00:04:44.688 --> 00:04:51.672
The three bars per group are the training, validation and test
splits, showing the balance is consistent across them.

00:04:52.122 --> 00:05:01.434
This is why they use class-weighted loss and minority
oversampling during training, and why they treat F1 rather

00:05:02.784 --> 00:05:07.152
They compare five models spanning the range from classical to
modern.

00:05:07.602 --> 00:05:19.050
Support vector machine with an RBF kernel, random forest, a
three-layer MLP, a one-dimensional ResNet with ten residual

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:28.116
ResNet wins with an AUROC of 0.8665. The transformer is second
at 0.8222.

00:05:28.566 --> 00:05:37.494
The interesting result is in the middle. Random forest and
SVM, both classical, beat the MLP — which is a neural network.

00:05:37.944 --> 00:05:51.096
So depth alone is not what helps. What helps is an
architecture suited to the signal: convolution over a time

00:05:52.446 --> 00:05:58.542
The ROC curves show the same ordering, with ResNet's curve
furthest toward the top-left corner.

00:05:58.992 --> 00:06:06.240
The right panel is a subtler point. It plots how much each
model's AUROC varies across the four thresholds.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:14.370
The deep models vary most; ResNet varies most of all. SVM and
random forest are the most stable.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:25.500
The authors read that two ways, and both are fair. Rigid
decision boundaries are less sensitive to a shifting cut-off,

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:34.230
The flip side is that a model whose behaviour changes
substantially with the threshold needs re-validating whenever

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:42.060
Across all four thresholds ResNet stays on top, and the
ranking of the five models barely changes.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:52.470
But the level does. AUROC is highest at 35 percent — 0.8665 —
and declines steadily to 0.8266 at 50.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:07:00.840
The model is most discriminative for the sickest patients,
which is convenient, because that is the group where the

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:08.874
It is also the group where a screening test would be most
useful: flagging patients whose EF is low enough to warrant

00:07:10.224 --> 00:07:14.448
One detail worth pausing on, because it is easy to misread.

00:07:14.898 --> 00:07:23.778
As the threshold rises, AUROC falls — from 0.8665 at 35
percent down to 0.8266 at 50.

00:07:24.228 --> 00:07:32.508
F1 does the opposite, climbing from 0.710 to 0.736. The two
lines cross.

00:07:32.958 --> 00:07:42.558
That is not a contradiction. AUROC is influenced by the
majority class, and at the 35 percent threshold the low-EF

00:07:43.008 --> 00:07:49.320
F1 balances precision and recall on the positive class, so
imbalance affects it far less.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:56.538
Reporting both, and being explicit about which is primary, is
the right call on a dataset this skewed.

00:07:57.888 --> 00:08:08.040
If EF could be estimated from one or two leads, this becomes
deployable on a wearable or a handheld device rather than a

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:21.882
Every single lead lands in a narrow band around 0.82 to 0.83
AUROC. Lead V2 is best at 0.8294, V3 worst at 0.8171.

00:08:22.332 --> 00:08:30.324
Leads I, aVL, aVF and V1 are consistently among the stronger
ones, so they test all six pairings of those four.

00:08:30.774 --> 00:08:41.214
The best pair, I and aVL, reaches 0.8354 — better than any
single lead, and still well below the 0.8665 from all twelve.

00:08:41.664 --> 00:08:50.904
The honest reading: no single lead and no pair is sufficient.
The twelve-lead combination carries information that no subset

00:08:52.254 --> 00:08:59.190
The 60-day window between ECG and echo is a design choice, so
they vary it from 10 to 60 days.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:06.072
Performance rises steadily with the window, peaking at 60 days
and the 35 percent threshold.

00:09:06.522 --> 00:09:16.386
There are two explanations and the paper is careful about
this. A wider window admits more training pairs, so part of

00:09:16.836 --> 00:09:23.244
It also weakens the pairing — an ECG two months from the echo
describes a heart that may have changed.

00:09:23.694 --> 00:09:29.262
Sixty days is where the dataset ran out, so it is not clear
the curve has plateaued.

00:09:30.612 --> 00:09:40.380
For interpretability they use integrated gradients, which
attributes a prediction back to each input sample by

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:48.174
This is one patient, correctly predicted as low EF. Red marks
the regions contributing most.

00:09:48.624 --> 00:09:55.944
The attribution concentrates on the QRS complex — the sharp
deflection produced by ventricular depolarisation.

00:09:56.394 --> 00:10:07.314
That is a coherent finding rather than a coincidence: QRS
morphology and duration reflect how the ventricles conduct and

00:10:07.764 --> 00:10:14.268
Contribution varies between patients, though, which is why
they also aggregate across the population.

00:10:15.618 --> 00:10:25.626
Summing attributions across the test set gives a per-lead
picture, broken down by outcome — true positives, true

00:10:26.076 --> 00:10:32.412
For predicting low EF, leads I, aVR, V2 and V3 carry most of
the weight.

00:10:32.862 --> 00:10:42.126
For predicting high EF, no lead stands out. The model appears
to be detecting the presence of abnormality rather than

00:10:42.576 --> 00:10:50.400
Combining this with the patient-level result: it is the QRS
region of those four leads that identifies low ejection

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:57.666
That is a testable, clinically legible claim, which is more
than most interpretability analyses produce.

00:10:59.016 --> 00:11:07.536
Summing the attributions across the test set gives a per-lead
picture, and it is easier to read on the standard twelve-lead

00:11:07.986 --> 00:11:13.074
Leads I and aVR carry the most weight for predicting low
ejection fraction.

00:11:13.524 --> 00:11:19.356
V2 and V3 follow. The remaining eight contribute substantially
less.

00:11:19.806 --> 00:11:28.782
And within those leads the attribution concentrates on the QRS
complex — the sharp deflection from ventricular

00:11:29.232 --> 00:11:38.088
That is physiologically coherent: QRS morphology reflects how
the ventricles conduct and contract, which is what ejection

00:11:39.438 --> 00:11:51.102
To summarise: a ten-second ECG carries enough information to
identify reduced ejection fraction at an AUROC of about 0.87,

00:11:51.552 --> 00:12:03.408
The best architecture was a one-dimensional ResNet, beating
both a transformer and a plain MLP — and the MLP lost to

00:12:03.858 --> 00:12:12.114
Interpretability points at the QRS region of leads I, aVR, V2
and V3, which is physiologically coherent.

00:12:12.564 --> 00:12:14.820
A few things to keep in view.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:22.374
There is no external validation — every result comes from one
hospital system, and the authors name this themselves.

00:12:22.824 --> 00:12:30.600
An AUROC near 0.87 supports triage, deciding who should get an
echo sooner, rather than replacing the echo.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:38.106
These are binary classifiers at chosen cut-offs, not EF
estimators, so the numbers move with the cut-off.

00:12:38.556 --> 00:12:51.228
And on fairness: the contribution here is studying a
